\name{BaseRCdplotLayerProcessor}
\alias{BaseRCdplotLayerProcessor}
\title{Base R Conditional Density Plot Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads \code{cdplot()} as the 100\% stacked area chart it draws.

A conditional density plot shows, for each value of a numeric \code{x}, how the
levels of a factor \code{y} divide up: the bands are stacked, they fill the
whole height, and their shares sum to 1 at every x. That is a normalized
stacked area, which \code{Ggplot2AreaLayerProcessor} already emits for
\code{position = "fill"}, so a \code{cdplot()} is read as
\code{stacked_normalized_area} and the two adapters describe one chart the
same way -- each series a list of \verb{\{x, y, z\}} where \code{y} is the band's own
share and \code{z} names the level, and one selector per band.

Before this, \code{cdplot()} had no branch in \code{detect_layer_type()}, so the
switch fell through to \code{"unknown"} and the chart degraded to a static
image (#216, #251).
\subsection{Where the curves come from}{

\code{cdplot()} has a \code{plot} argument, so it can be asked for what it drew
without drawing it again. It returns the \strong{boundaries} between the bands:
\code{nlevels(y) - 1} functions, each \code{approxfun()} over the density grid,
named for the level \emph{below} the boundary.

\preformatted{
rval <- cdplot(x, y, plot = FALSE)
names(rval)          # "c" "b"   for a factor with levels a, b, c
rval[[1]](50)        # the cumulative share at x = 50
}

Stacking them the way \code{cdplot()} does -- \code{rbind(0, boundaries, 1)}, band
\code{i} running from row \code{i} to row \code{i + 1} -- gives the shares back. Measured
over three hundred random charts, every column's shares sum to 1 and no
band comes out negative, so the boundaries do not cross in the drawn
range.

\code{graphics::cdplot} by the qualified name, not the bare one: maidr patches
the name on the search path, and a bare call would record the replay as a
second chart. The same line \code{BaseRSpineplotLayerProcessor} draws.
}

\subsection{Which x values were drawn}{

The returned functions interpolate over the density grid, which
\code{stats::density()} pads past the data by three bandwidths. \code{cdplot()}
throws that padding away before drawing:

\preformatted{
y1 <- y1[, which(x1 >= min(x) & x1 <= max(x))]
x1 <- x1[x1 >= min(x) & x1 <= max(x)]
}

so the announced grid is trimmed the same way -- 372 of the 512 points on
a measured chart. Announcing the untrimmed grid would put readings either
side of the data at x values the chart has no marks at.

The grid itself is read off the first returned function rather than
recomputed: \code{approxfun()} keeps its knots, and \code{environment(f)$x} is the
grid \code{cdplot()} built. Recomputing \code{density()} here would have to guess
\code{bw}, \code{n}, \code{from}, \code{to} and \code{weights} back out of the recorded call, and
a guess that differed by one point would shift every reading.
}

\subsection{What the replay proves}{

A \code{cdplot()} that returned rather than stopping has already established
most of what a reader here would otherwise have to check, because
\code{cdplot()} checks it first and stops. Each was measured:

\itemize{
\item \code{y} is a factor -- "dependent variable should be a factor";
\item \code{x} is finite -- \code{stats::density()} stops on a missing or
infinite value;
\item a formula names exactly two variables -- "'formula' should
specify exactly two variables";
\item the grid overlaps the data -- a \code{from}/\code{to} that put it elsewhere
stops with "need at least two non-NA values to interpolate";
\item the factor has at least two levels -- one stops with "subscript
out of bounds".
}

The NULL checks that remain below are therefore not checking any of that.
They are there so that a shape none of the above rules out cannot \emph{throw}
out of the pipeline, where declining to read leaves the static image the
figure produces today. Where a check would only repeat one another check
already makes, it is not written twice.
}
}
