\subsection{\code{BaseRCdplotLayerProcessor$band_levels()}}{
  The band names, bottom to top

\code{cdplot()} names its boundaries for every level but the topmost, so
the one it does not name is the one level of the response that is
left. Derived that way rather than by reproducing the \code{ylevels}
argument's reordering, which is \code{cdplot()}'s to define.

Declines unless exactly one level is left over. That is the one
guard the step needs, and it does double duty: an \code{ylevels} naming a
strict subset of the factor's levels leaves two over -- measured,
\code{ylevels = c("a", "b")} on a three-level factor -- and unnamed
boundaries would leave every level over. Either way the reading
would be missing a band, and every selector after it would point at
its neighbour.

An empty level name is \emph{not} declined. \code{factor(x, levels = c("b", ""))} is a level like any other, \code{setdiff()} matches it like any
other, and the chart draws a band for it -- so it is announced with
the empty name the axis shows rather than dropped.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{BaseRCdplotLayerProcessor$band_levels(boundaries, args)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
